      "prompt": "B. Draft an English abstract115–145words and a contribution≤40words from this fictional contract only. Approved English proposed name MaskRouteFD; no approved Chinese full name, novelty comparison pending. Task is known-class bearing fault classification in an IIoT service. Before evaluation, one CNN parent is trained and three variants are obtained by fixed parameter masks and individual fine-tuning; their classification accuracies are estimated on a declared development/calibration subset, never evaluation labels. Their weights/masks remain fixed during evaluation. A learned scheduler observes available queue and wireless-link state, selects one of the cached local variants or routes the task to a full parent on an edge server, and allocates edge computing capacity. The chosen model supplies the fault-class decision. The objective is timely task completion subject to estimated model-accuracy requirements. Two held-out simulated IIoT traffic scenarios, replaying recorded bearing signals, yield a lower simulated late-task fraction than always running the full parent locally. The simulator assumes inference cost proportional to retained nonzero weights; no device latency, sparse serialization saving or field deployment was measured. No online CNN fitting, per-sample correctness guarantee, causal or cross-plant result exists. Keep model preparation and online scheduling distinct, link the actual operations to fault decisions and the stated comparison, and carry the modeled-cost/simulation scope naturally in the prose rather than inventing empirical hardware benefits or a long defensive closing checklist. Do not make ordinary pruning independently novel; preserve the proposed design candidate while putting novelty verification in author notes. No invented graph, temporal/frequency branch, new data/result or modules. State exact word counts.",
